<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  pom.xml  -  "Project Object Model"
  ============================================================================
  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL:
  Maven is a build tool. A build tool needs to be told three things before it
  can do anything: (1) what this project is called, (2) what other people's
  code this project needs, and (3) how to turn the source files into a
  runnable program. This file is the answer to all three, written down in XML
  so a machine can read it. If you delete this file, the folder stops being a
  Maven project entirely - "mvn" will refuse to run and say it cannot find a
  POM in this directory.

  It is XML and not Java because it describes the project, it is not part of
  the project. Nothing in here ever runs as part of your application.
  ============================================================================
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <!--
      The three attributes above tell an XML editor which vocabulary of tags is
      legal inside this file, so your IDE can autocomplete tag names and
      underline typos in red. They do not change the build in any way. Maven
      itself ignores them. Leave them exactly as they are.
    -->

    <!--
      The version of the POM file FORMAT itself - not of Maven, and not of our
      project. It has been "4.0.0" since 2005 and is still 4.0.0 for Maven 3.
      It is required, and there is currently no other legal value for us.
    -->
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!--
      ========================================================================
      1. THE PARENT
      ========================================================================
      WHY: a "parent" POM is another pom.xml, published on the internet, whose
      settings this file inherits - exactly like a Java class extending another
      class. We inherit from Spring Boot's official parent.

      What we inherit, concretely:
        - A giant list of "if you ask for library X, use version Y" rules,
          covering roughly 400 libraries that Spring tested together. This is
          why every <dependency> below has NO <version> tag: the parent already
          decided, and every chosen version is known to work with every other.
        - Sensible defaults for the Java compiler, file encoding (UTF-8), and
          the plugins that do the actual building.
        - Configuration for the spring-boot-maven-plugin declared at the bottom.

      WHAT BREAKS WITHOUT IT: every dependency below would need its own
      <version>, and you would personally be responsible for picking versions of
      Spring, Tomcat, Jackson, Logback and JUnit that do not fight each other.
      That job is a full afternoon and is easy to get subtly wrong.
    -->
    <parent>
        <!-- The organisation that publishes the parent. Written in reverse
             internet-domain order (a worldwide convention that guarantees two
             companies never accidentally claim the same name). -->
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <!-- The specific artifact within that organisation. -->
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
        <!-- THE PINNED VERSION FOR THIS ENTIRE COURSE. Changing this one number
             changes the version of Spring, Tomcat, Jackson, Logback, JUnit and
             ~400 other libraries all at once, because they are all decided by
             this parent. Verified present on Maven Central on 2026-09-30. -->
        <version>4.1.1</version>
        <!-- WHY THIS EMPTY TAG: by default Maven first looks for the parent POM
             in the folder ABOVE this one, on disk. Ours is not there - it is on
             the internet. An empty <relativePath/> means "do not look on disk,
             go straight to the repository". Without it Maven prints a warning
             on every single build. -->
        <relativePath/>
    </parent>

    <!--
      ========================================================================
      2. WHO WE ARE  (the "coordinates" of this project)
      ========================================================================
      Three values that together form a globally unique address for this
      project, the same way groupId + artifactId + version identify every
      library we depend on. Our project is itself a library as far as Maven is
      concerned - it just happens to be the one we are writing.
    -->
    <!-- Our organisation. Reverse-domain convention again. -->
    <groupId>com.ecommerce</groupId>
    <!-- The project's short name. This becomes part of the built file name. -->
    <artifactId>ecommerce</artifactId>
    <!-- Our own version. "SNAPSHOT" is a Maven keyword meaning "still being
         worked on, expect this to change" - as opposed to a released version
         like 1.0.0 which must never change once published. -->
    <version>0.0.1-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <!-- How to package the compiled result. "jar" = one single executable file.
         The old alternative, "war", produced a file you had to copy into a
         separately installed Tomcat. We want the embedded server, so: jar. -->
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <!-- Human-readable labels. They appear in build logs and in generated
         documentation. They have no effect on compilation. -->
    <name>ecommerce</name>
    <description>E-Commerce platform - Phase 1 monolith</description>

    <!--
      ========================================================================
      3. PROPERTIES  (named values reused elsewhere in the build)
      ========================================================================
      A <properties> block declares variables. Anything declared here can be
      referenced later as ${the-name}. More importantly, setting a property that
      the PARENT already declared overrides the parent's value - which is
      exactly what we are doing here.
    -->
    <properties>
        <!-- Spring Boot's parent sets java.version to 17 by default. We raise
             it to 21 because 21 is the Long Term Support release this course
             pinned in Lesson 00. The parent uses this value for the compiler's
             "release" setting, so this single line is what makes javac compile
             against Java 21 and refuse Java 22+ syntax. -->
        <java.version>21</java.version>
    </properties>

    <!--
      ========================================================================
      4. DEPENDENCIES  (other people's code that we need)
      ========================================================================
      Each <dependency> is a request: "fetch this library and put it on the
      classpath". Maven fetches it, then fetches everything IT needs, then
      everything THOSE need, and so on, all the way down. That cascade is called
      transitive dependency resolution, and it is the reason two entries below
      are enough to assemble roughly forty jar files.
    -->
    <dependencies>

        <!--
          ------------------------------------------------------------------
          THE WEB STARTER
          ------------------------------------------------------------------
          WHY: this one line is what makes our project a web application. It
          pulls in, transitively:
            spring-boot-starter          - core Boot: auto-configuration,
                                           logging, YAML reading
            spring-boot-starter-jackson  - turning Java objects into JSON
            spring-boot-starter-tomcat   - the EMBEDDED Tomcat server itself
            spring-boot-http-converter   - converting HTTP bodies to/from Java
            spring-boot-webmvc           - Spring MVC plus its auto-configuration

          NOTE THE NAME. Every tutorial written before 2026 says
          "spring-boot-starter-web". In Spring Boot 4 the correct name is
          "spring-boot-starter-webmvc". The old name still resolves but is
          deprecated and will be deleted in a future release. Explained in full
          in Lesson 02.

          NO <version> TAG: the parent already decided it (4.1.1).
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-webmvc</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          ------------------------------------------------------------------
          THE WEB TEST STARTER
          ------------------------------------------------------------------
          WHY: everything needed to write automatic tests for a Spring MVC
          application. It pulls in, transitively:
            spring-boot-starter-test     - JUnit 5, AssertJ, Mockito, Spring's
                                           own test support
            spring-boot-starter-jackson-test - JSON assertions
            spring-boot-webmvc-test      - MockMvc, for calling controllers
                                           without starting a real server
            spring-boot-resttestclient   - RestTestClient, the new HTTP test
                                           client in Boot 4

          NOTE THE NAME AGAIN. Pre-2026 tutorials say "spring-boot-starter-test".
          That artifact still exists and is still the core of testing - but in
          Boot 4 the web-specific test helpers live in the per-technology
          starter "spring-boot-starter-webmvc-test", which includes
          spring-boot-starter-test inside it. Ask for the webmvc one and you get
          both.

          <scope>test</scope> means: put this on the classpath when compiling
          and running TESTS, but leave it out of the final jar. Test libraries
          have no business shipping to production - they would bloat the file
          and expose testing tools to the outside world.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-webmvc-test</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

    </dependencies>

    <!--
      ========================================================================
      5. BUILD  (which plugins actually do the work)
      ========================================================================
      Maven itself does almost nothing. Every real action - compiling, running
      tests, zipping the jar - is performed by a plugin. Most of them are
      configured for us by the parent and never need mentioning. One does.
    -->
    <build>
        <plugins>
            <!--
              WHY: a plain Maven build produces a jar containing only OUR
              compiled classes. Running it would fail instantly, because none of
              the forty Spring and Tomcat jars are inside it. Java has no
              built-in way to nest one jar inside another and still load it.

              This plugin solves that. It takes the ordinary jar and repackages
              it into a "fat jar" (also called an uber jar or executable jar):
              our classes, every dependency jar, and a small purpose-built
              loader that knows how to read nested jars - all in one file that
              runs with "java -jar".

              It also provides the "spring-boot:run" goal, which starts the
              application straight from source without packaging first. That is
              the command we use during development.

              No <version> here either: the parent pinned it to 4.1.1 too.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
